= Discussion

While some comparisons have been made here between all the batteries in general, and in comparison to the NiMH battery that was also discharged, the vast majority of comparison within this discussion section was done between successive iterations and within a single iteration. As mentioned before, no data was collected for iteration 1, due to a lack of reliability and standardization in the data.

@Pot2 through @C2-D3 describe the discharges of the second battery. The most striking feature of @Pot2 appeared to be the high amount of correlation between discharges 2 and 3. The mean and median values of discharges 2 and 3 lie within 5% of each other, while the change in potential (represented by the $Delta y$ on the statistics of each curve), shows greater variation. The curves all seemed to be converging onto a similar value, and an exponential function was found to be a good model of the battery behavior for $t>30$ min. However, for $t<30$ min, the battery potential dropped at a steeper rate than could be modeled for an exponential function. The most likely explanation for this is the electrical double layer capacitance explained earlier; ions adsorb onto the activated carbon very quickly, allowing for higher initial discharge currents, but this process cannot sustain for long. 

It is therefore likely that the intercalation process that should dominate the discharge after a long time has passed (in this case, most likely when $t>30$ min) exhibits exponential decay. This can be explained using rate kinetics. The chemical equation governing the oxidation of zinc was shown in @zinc-ox. This equation is an elementary reaction (as nothing simpler would be possible), and so the reaction order can be determined from the stoichiometric coefficient of 1. The intercalation equation of zinc into graphite was shown in @interc-eq, but is shown here again for clarification:

$ "Zn"^(2+)"(aq)" + x ce("C")(s) + 2e^- --> "ZnC"_x (s) $

It can be safely assumed that this @interc-eq is an elementary reaction, as no simpler reaction is possible. As graphite (C) and the zinc-graphite intercalation compound ($"ZnC"_x$) are both solids, the reaction must be first order and based on the concentration of zinc ions. The general equation for first order kinetics can be described through the following differential equation:

$ (dif C)/(dif t) = -k C $ <rate-dif>

$ C = C_0 e^(-k t) $ <rate-eq>

In @rate-dif, $C$ represents the concentration (in this case, of zinc ions) and $k$ is the rate constant for the specific reaction. The solution to this differential equation is well known and can be seen in @rate-eq. However, adding together the anode and cathode equations gives @total-eq, and simplification down into @net-eq shows that the overall concentration of zinc-ions does not change. Therefore, @rate-eq can only be applied over a localized area, which would most likely be around the anode or cathode. A local concentration gradient could heavily impede electrical conductivity, leading to the similar (but not quite) to exponential decay of voltage visible in @Pot2.


$ "Zn"(s) + "Zn"^(2+)(s) + x ce("C") + 2e^- --> "Zn"^(2+) + 2e^- + "ZnC"_x $ <total-eq>

$ "Zn"(s) + x ce("C") --> "ZnC"_x $ <net-eq>

Since iteration 2 was discharged 3 times at #qty(100, "ohm"), the currents measured were all very low; so low, in fact, that the current sensor could barely pick up many of the fluctuations. The very low current led to what looks like horizontal lines on the graphs of @C2-D1, @C2-D2, and @C2-D3, as the current probe reached its maximum precision. The maximum current of iteration 2, as visible in the statistics of @C2-D3, was 0.01049 A at $t=0$ min during the third discharge. The integrals of the graphs shown in @C2-D1, @C2-D2, and @C2-D3 show the total amount of charge pumped through the battery over the 8 hours. While @C2-D3 shows the highest current, the greatest charge capacity can be seen in @C2-D2, as the integral of @C2-D2 is the greatest, at $3.079 unit("A min") = 184.7 unit("C") = 51.32 "mAh"$. A standard AA NiMH battery has a useful charge capacity of 1100 mAh, while the maximum output from the second battery in a single discharge was about 21.43 times less. However, the much lower quality and simpler designs used in this experiment when compared with a commercial battery built to maximize energy density can account for this massive difference in charge capacity.

@Ch2 shows the emf v. time for each charge of the second battery. As mentioned before, the emf is equivalent to the open-circuit voltage, so the circuit was opened with a timer to find the emf on regular intervals. For all 3 charges shown in @Ch2, the maximum emf was reached at the end of the charge, as is to be expected. It can also be seen that the higher the initial emf was, the smaller the overall change in emf was as well. The emf of a battery will approach that of the power supply, similar to how the voltage drop across a capacitor approaches the voltage of a power source. If while charging, the battery is approximated as a capacitor, the following equation for the voltage of a capacitor can be used to estimate the voltage of the battery:

$ V  = V_"max" (1 - e^(-t/tau)) $ <cap-volts>

In @cap-volts, _V_ is the voltage at any given time, $V_"max"$ is the maximum voltage across the battery (which is equivalent to the cell potential), _t_ is the time, and $tau$ is the time constant for the equation. However, the curve fits of @Ch2 are of the following form:

$ V = A e^(-C t) + B $ <volt-exp>

However, @volt-exp can be rewritten in the format of @cap-volts if a time offset is inserted into @cap-volts, as shown in the following derivation:

$ V = B thin (1 - (-A/B) thin e^(-C t)) $
$ V = B thin (1 - e^(-C t + ln(-A/B))) $

Substituting $V_"max"$ for _B_, $1/tau$ for _C_, and $t_0/tau$ for $ln(-A/B)$ creates the following equation:

$ V = V_"max" thin (1-e^(-(t-t_0)/tau)) $ <exp-offset>

The only difference between @exp-offset and @volt-exp is the addition of a time offset. However, $V_"max"$ can be calculated from the equations shown on the graphs through the values of _B_. Thus, for the second battery, the predicted maximum emfs from the equations are 0.9885 V, 1.056 V, and 1.018 V. However, the statistics from @Ch2 show that the maximum emf of the battery during charge 2 was 1.061 V, which is slightly higher than the predicted value. This shows the limitations of modeling a battery as a capacitor and that the exponential model may not account for variations in the battery rising from the chemical reactions occurring to generate the cell potential.



Similar to the graph of potential difference v. time shown in @Pot2, the graph shown in @Pot3 shows a much lower initial potential during the first discharge than the others. Since the battery was not charged before the first discharge, this is to be expected. Discharge 1 was performed at 10 ohms, so the very steep drop-off in terminal voltage is expected, as the internal resistance rises quicker when the current is higher. It seems that something happened to the third battery around 1.5 hours after discharge 3 began, as there is much greater variation in that area. This variation is most likely caused by some outside disturbance that causes a change in the battery. For example, if some shaking in the environment had caused the cathode and anode to move closer together, the distance ions would need to travel to complete the circuit would decrease, decreasing the internal resistance and thus increasing the terminal voltage. It seems that as the batteries went through dischage-charge cycles, the terminal voltage over time seems to smoothen. This can be seen with the in the second discharge graph and the discharge 3 graph (apart from around $t=90$ min). The discharge 1 graph seems much more rough, which is likely due to the higher discharge currents and the "break-in" effect mentioned before, where battery performance can improve after a discharge.

@C3-D1 shows the current in the third battery during the first discharge. Similar to what was mentioned for the terminal voltage of the first discharge, there was very steep drop-off in current within the first hour of the discharge. More surprising at the time was the very low current value, which the statistics for @C3-D1 show to reach a max of 0.05 A. However, as was seen in @Pot3, this is due to the low potential difference that the third battery had during its first discharge.

@C3-D2 is almost impossible to interpret due to the tiny currents which bordered on the edge of the precision of the current probe. The current was so low that because of the limit of precision on the current probe, the minimum value for @C3-D2 reads a negative value. This is to be expected, because the battery was disharge across a 1000 ohm resistor. With such high resistance, a very low current is expected, leading to the difficult to interpret results of @C3-D2.

During discharge 3, as shown in @C3-D3, the third battery performed much better, as the integral of @C3-D3 was found to be greater than that of @C3-D1, even though the external resistance was 10 times larger. From this, it can be inferred that the third battery's performance improved considerably after charging and with a few "break-in" cycles done first. If another 10 ohm discharge had been done for this battery, it is likely that the maximum current and charge capacity would have been much higher.

As mentioned in the procedure, the cathode of Iteration 3 was placed into zinc sulfate solution instead to test the performance obtained from mixing the two electrolytes. This is shown in @Pot3b and @C3b. By far, the most noticeable effect in both graphs is the extremely steep slope of the curve at the beginning, followed by a somewhat constant value afterward. In @Pot3b, the statistics show that the average voltage was 0.08880 V, while @C3b shows that the average current was 1.617 mA. Such a low potential and current implies that the energy stored in the modified Iteration 3 battery must have been a small quantity and left the battery very quickly. When there are more available sites for intercalation in the graphite cathode, the potential difference is larger. Thus, from this graph, it can be inferred that the amount of intercalation reactions occuring was very limited. Thus, if the performance of the modified Iteration 3 battery were to be improved, charging the battery for an extended period of time before a discharge test may have produced better results. However, due to the extreme drop in voltage and current that occurred in this battery almost immediately, it was deemed unnecessary to expand research on this battery, as its performance was far poorer than all of the rest of the batteries.

@Ch3 shows the emfs while charging iteration 3. Similar to @Ch2, the battery emf increased over time, approaching the value of the power supply. From the curve fits of @Ch3, the expected maximum emf values are 1.338 V for the first charge and 1.466 V for the second charge. These values are much higher than the respective values for iteration 2, which most likely results from the switch of electrolyte. However, the exponential curve does not correlate as well with the first charge. A possible cause for the spikes seen on the graph may be a misaligned timing circuit, causing the voltage to be read while the circuit was closed. If that was the case, then those values show the power supply voltage at those times. However, the spikes would be expected to happen more often than twice, with a 15 minute gap in between. The power supply voltage possibility also does not explain the much lower voltage in at the next data point that was collected, unless also caused by a misalignment of the timing circuit. 

@C3-C can be analyzed in a similar fashion to @C2-C, though there seems to be much more of a trend in the charges of iteration 3. The current does start higher and end up lower, though there is considerable variation in the current during the process as well. The current in this case can be used as a metric of the reaction rate: the higher the current, the faster the reverse reactions are happening, causing the battery to recharge. Thus, since the reactions were modeled in @rate-eq to follow exponential decay, in the same way, the current show exponential decay. As there is significant variation within @C3-C, there are likely some other processes which have a significant (if not dominant) effect on the current as time passes.

@Pot4, similarly to @Pot2 and @Pot3, shows the first discharge to have a much lower potential than the other two, for the same reasons as explained before. However, in both @Pot3 and @Pot4, the second discharge started and stayed at a higher potential than the third discharge, while this effect was not visible in @Pot2. While this is most obviously due to the 10 times lower resistance in the third discharge of each battery, another factor could be in the battery chemistry. In the third and fourth iteration of the battery, zinc chloride was used in place of zinc sulfate. The chloride ion is much more reactive than the sulfate ion, so even if no precipitates were to form, the greater amount of side reactions (especially during recharging) could lower the voltage and current of the battery, even only after 2 discharges.

The most amount of charge pumped through any battery can be seen in @C4-D1, with the total ouput being an incredible 2.818 Ah. This is almost 55 times better than the best discharge of iteration 2. However, even more surprising was the max output current of over 1 A, which should not be possible through a 10 ohm resistor with a 1.5 V battery. Using the maximum terminal voltage for discharge from @Pot4 (the maximum voltage and current both occured at $t=0$ min), Ohm's Law can be used to calculate the external resistance, leading to the following equations:

$ V = I R $
$ R = V/I=(0.09371 unit("V"))/(1.009 unit("A"))=0.09287 unit("ohm") $ <4-1-res>

From @4-1-res, it can be seen that this battery must have short-circuited, as #qty(0.09287, "ohm") is much more likely to be the total resistance of all of the wires. Unfortunately, a battery that is able to short circuit and be completely fine means that the internal resistance of the battery must have been the only load for the battery's current. Assuming that the cell potential was around 1.5 V, the internal resistance would followed this calculation:

$ V=epsilon-I r $
$ r = (epsilon - V)/I=(1.5 unit("V") - 0.0937 unit("V"))/(1.009 unit("A"))=1.394 unit("ohm") $ <short-r>

While this value is quite good, the value given in @short-r is still orders of magnitude larger than that of a commercial battery (which is why those batteries should not be short-circuited).

@C4-D2, similar to @C3-D2, cannot be analyzed effectively, as the current values are so small that the graph is essentialy at a constant value. However, @C4-D3 shows much more useful results, as this discharge did not involve a very high or very low resistance. These values are very similar to that of @C3-D3, yet the maximum current of @C4-D3 was smaller while the charge capacity was higher. This can be explained through the use of much more graphite powder in the construction of iteration 4, causing more intercalation and less adsorption. More adsorption caused the current in @C3-D3 to be higher, while more intercalation caused the total amount of charge pumped through the battery in @C4-D3 to be higher, as intercalation is a far slower process that results in steadier currents, while adsorption relies on the electrical double layer to produce a capacitative effect.

It is difficult to interpret @Ch4, as the emfs do not follow any one trend. Thus, unlike @Ch2 and @Ch3, no curve fits were done for @Ch4. The first charge shows that sudden spikes that were visible in @Ch3, yet it is sustained for a little while. Again, this can be put down to misalignment of the timing circuit and the timing of the data collection. Other than those three values, however, the emf in charge 1 seems to steadily increase. However, the emf in charge 2 shows a slightt decrease over time, with the maximum value being the very first emf measured. The most plausible reasons for this are that the battery was already charged to the voltage of the power supply or the current dropped to 0 for one reason or another. The first answer is unlikely, as the other two discharges easily surpass the emf values shown in scharge 2. Thus, the current most likely dropped to close to 0.

That current drop for charge 2 is evident in @C4-C, as the current value went from a max of 0.1062 A down to around 0.002708 A after the first data point. The second data point was recorded 1 minute after the first, so something must have occurred within that time to bring the current close to 0. If a short-circuit had occurred somewhere in the circuit, it is possible that the power supply detected that short-circuit and set the current to almost 0 in order to prevent any damage from occurring via that short circuit. The graps for charges 1 and 2 look more regular, but again, there is no clear exponential trend. There are random spikes and drops in the current, though the overall trend is clear: some sort of decay function, trending towards 0.

The most interesting part of @Pot5 was the inflection point visible on the graph, which is characteristic of many standard battery discharge graphs. In a commercial battery, the potential stays at close to the nominal value for a long time, and only drops at the very end of the battery's lifetime. Something similar can be seen here in both discharges 1; the potential stayed above 1.8 V for 40 minutes before the reaching the inflection point and switching from a battery-like discharge to a capacitor-like discharge (exponential decay). 

The timespan of @Pot5, as well as @C5-D1 and @C5-D2, was greatly limited due to adverse effects while charging the battery. Due to the higher potential at which Iteration 5 was charged, chlorine gas began to form and reached a high enough concentration in the air to become noticeable. To mitigate the adverse effects of chlorine gas, a door was opened to allow fresh air from the outside to come in. The temperature had been kept constant at #qty(18.5, "celsius"), but allowing air from the outside caused the temperature to drop to #qty(5.3, "celsius"). Batteries perform worse at lower temperatures due to decreased reaction rates, causing a steep drop in the potential and current of the battery. As these effects were most noticeable later in the discharges, it was decided to cut the data off at $t=90$ minutes to ensure the data would be comparable to the other iterations, while the effects from the production of chlorine gas rendered the charging graph unreliable and not fit to use.

@C5-D1 is another example where the small current values began to reach the limit of the current probe's precision. As expected from the much smaller timespan and very small current, the charge pumped through the iteration 5 battery was much smaller than many of the values of iterations 2-4. However, @C5-D2 shows much higher current values and the inflection point (the "hump") that is only seen in battery discharge graphs. Neverthless, the current through the battery becomes so low as to be negligible after even just 1 hour. It is likely that this is due to the general cooling of the environment caused by the opening of the door. Even in this short time, the fifth iteration battery was able to pump a considerable amount of charge ($2.106 unit("A min") = 126.36 unit("C") = 35.1 "mAh"$). Thus, by combining the changes made to iteration 4 and iteration 5 along with other design changes, considerable improvement could be made in future experiments to produce a battery which can compete with many of the current commercial batteries.

@PotS and @CS show the voltage and current while discharging an NiMH battery across a 1000 ohm resistor. With such a high resistance (and therefore a very low current), it was not surprising that the NiMH battery voltage showed almost no drop over the 8 hour discharge. However, from @CS, the total charge pumped by this battery was 0.4568#unit("A min"). While the other two 1000 ohm discharge graphs, @C3-D2 and @C4-D2, did not have quite as high of a value, @C4-D2 got quite close with 0.4180#unit("A min"). Nonetheless, this considerable gap between the 8 hour charge capacities show that the current through the zinc-ion batteries was quite hampered by the much higher internal resistance, which still had a considerable impact even when the external resistance was so large.

The points for discharges 2 and 3 are almost indistinguishable in @P2, which can be explained for the same reasons as to why the two discharges were so close in @Pot2 and between @C2-D2 and @C2-D3. The energy stored by a battery is generally measured in mWh for smaller batteries like those made here. The total energy output from discharge 2 of iteration 2 (and thus, approximately the energy output from discharge 3) was $2.055 "W min" = 34.25 "mWh"$. Using the effective charge capacity of a NiMH mentioned before and the voltage of an NiMH battery, the total effective energy of a NiMH battery is as follows:

$ 1100 "mAh" times 1.2 unit("V") = 1320 "mWh" $ <nrg-imh>

As can be seen in @nrg-imh, the energy stored in a single AA battery is far greater than what has been produced here. This does not even account for the effective capacity of the batteries, which will be discussed later. Nonetheless, there is substantial room for improving the quality and design of zinc-ion batteries, as can be seen from these numbers.

The results for @P3 are not much better, though there is an interesting extreme spike in power that can be seen at the very beginning of the first discharge. This could be due to a high starting current which rapidly drops off in @C3-D1. Surprisingly, the total energy output in discharge 1 and discharge 2 are almost identical, even though discharge 1 has the much higher initial output. However, this is most likely due to the much steadier current and voltage in discharge 2 due to the 1000 ohm resistance. None of the discharges of iteration 3 produced as much power as discharges 2 and 3 of iteration 2.

Due to the short-circuit visible in @C4-D1, the energy output of discharge 1 of iteration 4 in @P4 is much greater than any of the other discharges. However, due to the extremely low terminal voltage, the actual power was far lower. If the terminal voltage had stayed at a much higher value (close to the cell potential), then the first discharge of iteration 4 would have had a greater total power output than a AA battery. The third discharge of iteration 4 also shows a considerable amount of energy released. With the higher loads, the batteries function much more like the constant voltage source a battery is supposed to be, due to the current being lower in @Vterm. Thus, at higher loads, the zinc-ion battery is able to produce a constant power output. However, that power output is very small; the average power output for discharge 3 in @P4 is 4.822 mW. Not much can be done with so little power.

In @P5, the power output from discharge 1 also appears to be relatively constant. This can again be attributed to the high load, which leads to small yet somewhat constant current. And again, this causes the voltage to stay about constant as well due to the effects of @Vterm. Nevertheless, the amount of energy released is quite low, even for discharge 2: $1.710 unit("W min") = 102.6 unit("J")$, which is about one-third of the energy needed to melt 1 g of ice (Engineering Toolbox, 2019).

The capacity and energy of the various batteries was discussed extensively earlier in this paper, but Tables 1-4 instead have the effective capacity and energy. As mentioned before, this value was found by only using taking the integral of the part of each discharge where the voltage was greater than 0.9 V. To make the quantities easier to understand, each value on the table was expressed in the derived units of the graphs and in standard SI units. 

In @cap2, the first discharge of the battery had no useful energy or capacity. This can be attributed to the fact that the battery was not charged before the first discharge, and so the potential difference within the battery was not large enough for the battery to have a useful capacity or energy. The effective capacity and energy for discharge 2 was very close to half of that for discharge 3. Looking at @Pot2, this can be explained because the battery reaches 0.9 V so quickly (within the first half hour) in discharge 2 that what seems to be a slightly longer time spent above 0.9 V for discharge 3 ends up having a massive effect.

@cap3 shows essentially the same effects, with the first discharge not having a high enough voltage for a capacity to be read, and the third discharge having close to double the values of the second discharge. This can be explained for the same reasons as for why these phenomena occured in @cap2. However, the use of zinc chloride in iteration 3 does not show a clear improvement, as the values shown for discharges 2 and 3 of @cap3 are about the same as the values shown in @cap2.

Like @cap2 and @cap3, @cap4 shows no effective charge capacity or energy in the first discharge. Since the battery short-circuited during the first discharge, the terminal voltage never came close to 0.9 V, even though the current was very high. The other two discharges in @cap4 also showed much less effective capacity and energy than those of @cap2 and @cap3. Thus, it is possible that adding the extra graphite powder in iteration 4 may have contributed to the worse power output and energy output in this iteration of the battery.

Lastly, discharge 2 in @cap5 had by far the largest effective capacity and energy of any discharge, even though these batteries were not discharged for the full 8 hours due to the formation of chlorine gas, as mentioned before. Therefore, it is fully possible that the final iteration of the battery could have had even larger values for the effective capacity and energy.

Normalizing the graphs of voltage and current allow for effective comparisons even when the starting values are quite different. Thus, the normalized graphs allow for effective comparisons across all iterations. In @Vnorm, all of the normalized voltages v. time for the first discharges of the batteries are shown. While iteration 2 and iteration 3 both started with low voltages in the first discharge, the normalized voltage of iteration 3 dropped much faster than that of iteration 2. In other words, the % loss of voltage was much greater for iteration 3 than for iteration 2 over the same time period. This is most likely due to the zinc chloride, which is more reactive and will produce higher currents, but cannot sustain those currents for as long. The iteration 5 battery is the only one that shows the "hump" that defines a battery. All of the rest of the curves are much more akin to a capacitor, except that an exponential curve fit does fit well, due to the multitude of factors that effect the discharge.

@Cnorm was created instead only at discharges performed with the same external resistance, but there did not seem to be much of a correlation here either. The shape of the graphs, especially that of iteration 3, discharge 1, looks much more like an inverse power function. If the discharge were dominated by second-order kinetics, then an inverse function should model the reaction rate and thus the discharge, but that model is also unable to effectively capture the situation. Due to the inability to create effective curve fits for these graphs, no curve fits were added to remove visual clutter. In the iteration 5 graph shown here, however, almost no "hump" can be seen. Instead, the iteration 5, discharge 2 graph looks like just another discharge graph, though it is much steeper than the others. Again, it is plausible that this was caused by the drop in temperature occurring while this final iteration of the battery was discharging.

The information provided in @start-r and @end-r allows for the effective comparison of the internal resistances of the different batteries during the different discharges. As expected, all of the @start-r values at a specific spot on the table are less than the value at the same location in @end-r. However, there does not seem to be much correlation between the starting and ending internal resistances of the batteries. For example, while the starting internal resistance was lowest for iteration 2 at the beginning of discharge 3, the lower ending internal resistance for the same iteration was in discharge 2. Some of these values were much closer to a regular battery, such as discharge 1 of iteration 3 and discharge 3 of iteration 4, but there does not seem to be much reason as to why. Discharge 2 of iteration 3 and 4, as well as discharge 1 of iteration 5, were all performed at 1000 ohms, and these were the highest values in those columns for the starting internal resistance. The 10 ohm resistance discharges, such as discharge 1 of iterations 3 and 4, as well as discharge 2 of iteration 5, showed much lower internal resistances. However, this does not explain the decreasing starting internal resistance over time, as visible in the "Iteration 2" column of @start-r. It is instead possible for the iteration 2 battery that repeated discharges at the same resistance allowed for battery to exhibit the "break-in" effect for the first few cycles. To verify this, however, would require many more cycles to see whether the effect would persist.

== Analysis of Error

Due to the various and complex interactions occurring within a battery, there are numerous factors which could have had a substantial effect on the results. These factors include the surrounding environment, instrument errors, unwanted side reactions, and material purity, among other things.

While the surrounding environment was kept almost constant for the duration of data collection, the nature of the environment itself may had an effect on the battery. All of the experimentation, up until iteration 5, was conducted within a climate-controlled room at #qty(18.5, "celsius") and 35% relative humidity. Even in this environment, however, certain compounds in the air, moisture, and possible movement and shaking of the surroundings may have had an effect on the batteries.

Instrumental errors are an inherent problem of every experiment and can occur due a variety of factors, including: the age of the equipment, defects in production, interactions with the environment, imprecision inherent in the equipment, as well as due to calibration errors. Using newer equipment and checking the equipment for proper function can mitigate the first two errors, but interactions with the environment are often many and hard to control. Keeping the environment consistent, however, can help to mitigate those errors. As the equipment used is not exact, there is only a certain level of precision that can be reached, leading to the limitations seen in all of the current graphs for the 1000 ohm discharges. The voltage probe has a resolution of 3.1 mV, which did not become an issue for this paper (Vernier, 2024). However, the current probe had a resolution of 0.31 mA, which was large enough to become a problem in the analyses of @C3-D2, @C4-D2, and @C5-D1. While no current probes of higher precision were available to be used in this experiment, making sure the equipment used is as precise as possible for the range of values that one will work with can help to mitigate the imprecision of measurement. While automated systems were used to time all of the data collection done in this paper, imprecision in the timing of those systems (such as the Arduino microcontroller or the LabQuest2) could also cause a slight misalignment of current and voltage data. However, as the data was collected for 1 hour or 8 hours, a mistimed reading would not be expected to have a substantial effect. The resistance box used for the majority of the experiment is also not exact, as the resistance values can vary by up to 1. Since these resistance values were not used for any direct calculations, this source of error was not expected to make a significant difference in the results.

In addition to the errors inherent in the instruments used, irreversible side reactions can become a major issue over longer time scales. These reactions happen mostly when charging, and are the primary reason for why batteries lose charge capacity over time. An example comes from the problems found in iteration 5 of this experiment. The formation of chlorine gas while charging takes chlorine out of the solution, lowering the concentration of chlorine and therefore the conductivity of the solution. That is why zinc sulfate is currently the preferred electrolyte, as it is less reactive while still quite effective. 

Along with unwanted chemical reactions, dendrites can form on the surface of the zinc while charging. While charging, zinc ions are converted back into zinc metal which essentially electroplate (or form a layer on top of) the existing zinc foil. However, this plating is not uniform, and is instead favored around irregularities on the surface of the foil. As zinc continues to build up around the irregularity, a dendrite forms. It is possible for these dendrites to connect the anode to the cathode and thus cause a short-circuit across the battery, drastically lowering the voltage and current measured. Though possible, this is unlikely, due to the thickness of the filter paper used to create the cathode acts as a separator and ensures that the two electrodes would not come into contact.

One of the largest factors on the efficiency of the batteries is the purity of the materials used. For example, as visible in @carbon, the activated carbon used in this experiment was described as "food-grade". While this is not a clear specification, it is obvious that "food-grade" activated carbon will be at a lower purity than "lab-grade" or "industrial-grade" activated carbon. By the same logic, the purity of the zinc foil, zinc sulfate, zinc chloride, graphite powder, and manganese dioxide likely had a substantial effect of the efficiency of the batteries. Those impurities lead to the side reactions and dendrites mentioned earlier, but could also cause degradation of the cathode by preventing effective intercalation and adsorption, among other things. 
